@* What a run recorded: the rows as a table, the same rows as a curve, and the two controls that
   act on them.

   One component because it is one control on the desktop. ResultsPanel is put in the console
   window and in both script editors, in the same place and with the same two tabs, so a script
   moved between them records the same way and looks like it does. Three copies of it here drifted
   into three different things — one with tabs, two with the table and the plot stacked and no way
   to see either of them large. *@

<div class="group">
    @* No caption. The tabs name what is under them, and a word above them would be a word saying
       what the thing below it already says. *@
    <div class="subtabs">
        <button class="@(_pane == Pane.Table ? "on" : "")" @onclick="@(() => _pane = Pane.Table)"
                title="The rows this run recorded.">Results table</button>
        <button class="@(_pane == Pane.Plot ? "on" : "")" @onclick="@(() => _pane = Pane.Plot)"
                title="The same rows as a curve, in the order they were recorded.">Plot</button>
    </div>

    @* Headings and all, before there is anything under them — which is what a ListView with its
       columns set shows from the moment the window opens, and the better thing to show: the table
       says what this run is going to record. A line of prose in its place meant the first row did
       not arrive in the table. The table arrived, with the row already in it, and everything under
       it moved. *@
    @if (_pane == Pane.Plot)
    {
        <Plot Columns="@Columns" Rows="@Rows" />
    }
    else
    {
        @* A box of a size, not a list that grows. The console beside it is a fixed frame with a
           scrollbar, and this is the same thing: a table that was three rows tall and then four
           would move the controls under it on every reading. *@
        <div class="scroll fill">
            <table class="results">
                <thead>
                    <tr>@foreach (var c in Columns) { <th>@c</th> }</tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    @foreach (var r in Ordered)
                    {
                        <tr>@foreach (var v in r.Values) { <td class="mono">@v</td> }</tr>
                    }
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
    }

    @* Only under the table. These act on the rows, and the rows are what the table is; under the
       plot they stood beside its own camera button, which saves the same readings as a picture,
       so the corner offered two Saves meaning two things. *@
    @if (_pane == Pane.Table)
    {
        <div class="row foot">
            <span class="spacer"></span>
            <button @onclick="OnSaveCsv" disabled="@(Rows.Count == 0)"
                    title="Save the recorded rows as a CSV file."><Icon Name="save" /> Save CSV</button>
            <button @onclick="OnClear" disabled="@(Rows.Count == 0)"
                    title="Discard the recorded rows and the curve. The log is left alone — it is the record of how these were produced."><Icon Name="clear" /> Clear Results</button>
        </div>
    }
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>The column headings, in order.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public IReadOnlyList<string> Columns { get; set; } = [];

    /// <summary>The rows, in the order they were recorded.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public IReadOnlyList<RecordedRow> Rows { get; set; } = [];

    /// <summary>
    /// Newest at the top, which is how a console reads: the reading you just took is the one you
    /// are looking for. A script's rows are a sweep and read in the order it swept.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public bool NewestFirst { get; set; }


    /// <summary>
    /// Saving is the owner's, not this pane's: what goes in the file is not always what is in the
    /// table. A console writes the timestamp in full rather than to the second, which is the
    /// precision it took the reading at.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback OnSaveCsv { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public EventCallback OnClear { get; set; }

    private enum Pane { Table, Plot }

    private Pane _pane = Pane.Table;

    private IEnumerable<RecordedRow> Ordered => NewestFirst ? Rows.Reverse() : Rows;
}
